        {
          "text": "[ ] **Used?**",
          "indent_level": 0,
          "line_number": 74,
          "source": "topic-set-paleontology-the-tyrannosaurus-rex-🆔20251018220848.md"
        },
        {
          "text": "**The *Nanotyrannus* Debate:** A fierce taxonomic dispute over whether certain small, slender fossils belong to a distinct species (*Nanotyrannus lancensis*) or are simply juvenile *T. rex* specimens. This debate has massive implications for our understanding of *T. rex* ontogeny and tyrannosaur diversity.",
          "indent_level": 0,
          "line_number": 78,
          "source": "topic-set-paleontology-the-tyrannosaurus-rex-🆔20251018220848.md"
        },
        {
          "text": "**Integument (Feathers vs. Scales):** While *T. rex's* ancestors were clearly feathered, fossilized skin impressions from *T. rex* itself show scales. This has led to the hypothesis that it was **secondarily scaly**, perhaps losing its feathers as it evolved a massive body size to better regulate heat, much like modern elephants.",
          "indent_level": 0,
          "line_number": 80,
          "source": "topic-set-paleontology-the-tyrannosaurus-rex-🆔20251018220848.md"
        },
        {
          "text": "**Paleopathology:** The study of injuries and diseases in fossils. Healed *T. rex* bite marks on prey and evidence of injuries (like broken and healed bones) on *T. rex* skeletons themselves provide direct, tangible evidence of their behavior and the brutal nature of their lives.",
          "indent_level": 0,
          "line_number": 81,
          "source": "topic-set-paleontology-the-tyrannosaurus-rex-🆔20251018220848.md"
        }
      ]
    },
